# collie-client — Collie (https://colliepwa.dev), a mobile web interface for
# terminal-based AI agents, installed on THIS machine. Client-side: no
# container, nothing deployed to a remote host.
#
# ⚠️  READ THIS FIRST. Collie is remote shell access to this machine from a
# phone, by design. Upstream's own security page opens with it: "a single
# Collie API call sends arbitrary keystrokes directly to a live terminal
# pane… treat the URL as a root login." With herdr-client deployed the chain
# is: phone → Collie → Herdr → every agent pane.
#
# Two settings below are the difference between "my tailnet" and "anyone who
# gets the URL": COLLIE_TRUSTED_USER, and running `collie pair` after the
# first start. Neither is optional in practice.

# --- Access control (set these) ---

# Your tailnet login. Collie rejects any request whose Tailscale-User-Login
# header is missing or does not match. WITHOUT IT Collie runs in open
# single-user mode and anyone on your tailnet who reaches the URL has full
# control of this machine.
#
# run.sh writes this into Collie's own .env. Note Collie reads its .env only
# at startup — `collie restart` after a change.
# Format: the email-shaped login Tailscale shows for you.
#COLLIE_TRUSTED_USER=you@example.com

# Records that you have read the warning above, so a deploy does not stop to
# ask. run.sh prompts once before the FIRST install only; set this when you
# deploy non-interactively (a script, a remote shell with no TTY).
# Format: 1 to acknowledge, unset otherwise.
#COLLIE_ACK_REMOTE_SHELL=1

# run.sh REFUSES to install while Tailscale Funnel is enabled anywhere on
# this machine. `tailscale serve` is tailnet-only; `tailscale funnel`
# publishes to the open internet, and upstream states there is no supported
# use case for running Collie over Funnel.
#
# The check is deliberately blunt — it looks for any funnel at all rather
# than trying to match one to Collie's backend port, because matching means
# parsing Tailscale's JSON without jq and a wrong parse there fails OPEN.
# Set this only after checking `tailscale funnel status` yourself and
# confirming the funnel is for something else.
# Format: 1 to proceed anyway, unset otherwise.
#COLLIE_ALLOW_EXISTING_FUNNEL=1

# --- Version pinning ---

# Which Collie release to install. "latest" tracks upstream, and CLEAN then
# runs `collie update` (which keeps the previous version staged for
# `collie update --rollback`).
#
# A pinned tag changes CLEAN's mechanics rather than just its target: the
# installer lays that version down BESIDE the existing one and flips
# `current`, which is the rescue path when the installed version is itself
# the broken thing.
# Format: a release tag like v1.8.0, or "latest".
#COLLIE_TAG=latest

# Where Collie installs. The installer writes only here and ~/.local/bin, and
# never asks for sudo.
# Format: an absolute path.
#COLLIE_INSTALL_DIR=$HOME/.local/share/collie

# --- Networking ---

# Port the bridge listens on. Loopback only — `tailscale serve` proxies to
# it. Change this only if something else already owns 8787, or if you run
# more than one Collie instance on this host.
# Format: a port number.
#COLLIE_PORT=8787

# Set this if you are deliberately fronting Collie with your own reverse
# proxy instead of Tailscale. It turns off run.sh's Tailscale preconditions
# AND stops Collie publishing anything — your proxy's auth becomes the only
# thing between a phone and your shell.
# Format: 1 to skip tailscale serve, unset otherwise.
#COLLIE_SKIP_SERVE=1
